{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'field.types.title'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	The type, asked before anything else ([XIV-163]).

	Every other question about a new field depends on the answer to this one, so
	it is asked on its own and the next page is built from what it says. What a
	`choice` field cannot work without and what a `phone` field may optionally be
	told have nothing in common; a single form carrying both is a form that is
	mostly wrong whatever gets picked.

	The list is the registry, filtered by whether this editor can ask the type's
	own questions (XIV-144). Nothing here is written down per type: a type
	registered tomorrow appears on this page, and one whose question nobody has
	built a control for does not appear at all rather than appearing broken.

	The line under each name is derived the same way: it is the options that
	type declares, so it says what the next page will ask about before anybody
	commits to it.
#}
{% block body %}
	{#
		What each option is called, in one place.

		§5.4 says the thing that stays per option, deliberately, is *drawing* it: a
		select of three fixed answers and a numbering pattern with a live preview
		have nothing in common but the question "may this type have one". Naming one
		is part of drawing it, so this map exists rather than a label on the
		capability interface, which would make core learn what a form looks like. The
		forms themselves carry each label inside the block that draws the control;
		this page has no controls, only names, which is why it is the one place a map
		is needed at all. Numbering is not on it because it is not on the form
		either: it has a page of its own and is taken up after the field exists.
	#}
	{% set optionLabels = {
		'autocomplete': 'field.autocomplete',
		'region': 'field.region',
		'choices': 'field.option_list',
		'module': 'field.points_at',
		'list': 'field.takes_from',
		'exclusive_within': 'field.exclusive_within',
		'max_length': 'field.max_length',
		'min': 'field.min',
		'max': 'field.max',
	} %}

	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<div>
				<h1 class="h3 mb-0">{{ 'field.types.title'|trans }}</h1>
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'field.types.intro'|trans({'%shape%': shape.label}) }}</p>
			</div>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('field_index', {module: module.key}) }}">{{ 'field.options.back'|trans }}</a>
		</div>

		<div class="row g-3">
			{% for key, type in addable %}
				<div class="col-md-4">
					<a class="card h-100 text-decoration-none link-body-emphasis"
					   href="{{ path('field_add_form', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id, type: key}) }}">
						<div class="card-body">
							<h2 class="h6 mb-1">{{ type.label }}</h2>
							<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0"><code>{{ key }}</code></p>
							{#
								What this type will be asked about, named from its own
								declarations rather than from a list kept here. An empty one
								is the ordinary case and says so: most types are the key, the
								label and the rules, and nothing else.
							#}
							{% set asks = settings[key]|default([]) %}
							{% if asks is not empty %}
								<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 mt-2">
									{{ 'field.types.asks'|trans }}
									{% for option in asks %}{{ optionLabels[option] is defined ? optionLabels[option]|trans : option }}{{ not loop.last ? ', ' }}{% endfor %}
								</p>
							{% endif %}
						</div>
					</a>
				</div>
			{% endfor %}
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
